  "body": "## What changed\n\nThe heavy step `app heavy: pocket3d_hsm` was red on `main`. This PR finds the commit and updates the pins with proof. It changes no product code.\n\n- `crates/app/tests/pocket3d_hsm.rs`:\n  - The Linux mesh pin changes from 6228 to 5232 facets.\n  - The count pin changes from (3158, 3130, 1141) to `BORE_PIN` = (4688, 4660, 1980) on Windows x86_64, Linux x86_64 and macOS arm64.\n  - The exactness tests also reject the reading from before the bump.\n- `docs/decisions/3886-pocket3d-hsm-pin.md`: the decision record.\n\n## The first bad commit\n\nThe first bad commit is the merge of PR #3802 (df899f029, the solidcore bump). I did not need a full bisect. Linux x86_64, release, with a print-only probe in the test (not committed):\n\n| Commit | Facets | (moves, planar, arcs) | Warnings | Result |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 940cc5d0e (parent of df899f029) | 6228 | (3158, 3130, 1141) | 0 | pass |\n| df899f029 (#3802 merge) | 5232 | (4688, 4660, 1980) | 0 | fail |\n| baad348f1 (`main`) | 5232 | (4688, 4660, 1980) | 0 | fail |\n\nOn `main` the Linux run fails first at the mesh pin (line 80, 5232 against 6228), as sn3ro reported. All other asserts before the count pin pass.\n\n## Why the new counts are correct\n\nPR #3802 changes no planner code. It changes `Cargo.lock`, `crates/kernel/Cargo.toml` and `geometry/src/step_diag.rs`. Only the mesh changes. Measured per level (planar feeds, arcs, planar feed length):\n\n| Level | Before (6228 facets) | After (5232 facets) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Z 15 | 397, 72, 334.31 mm | 1933, 918, 999.92 mm |\n| Z 10 | 1933, 918, 999.92 mm | 397, 72, 334.31 mm |\n| Z 5 | 403, 79, 327.81 mm | 1933, 918, 999.92 mm |\n| Z 0 | 397, 72, 334.31 mm | 397, 72, 334.31 mm |\n\nEach level after the bump takes one of the two plans that the planner already made before the bump. The new mesh changes which levels take the long plan. It does not make a new plan. After the bump:\n\n- every level verifies clean against the part solid (no warnings);\n- no level falls back to rings;\n- no planning budget note shows;\n- the levels are Z 15, 10, 5 and 0.\n\nThere is no gouge.\n\nThe long plan cuts about 3 times the feed of the short plan for the same disc. Its feeds also stop 0.153 mm short of the wall in -Y. This defect is older than the bump: the old pin already held it at Z 10. It is a planner algorithm defect (`effort:max`), so this PR does not fix it. It is filed as #4010 (P3).\n\n## Platforms\n\n- Linux x86_64: measured in this work (table above).\n- Windows x86_64: the issue comment of 2026-11-24 measured (4688, 4660, 1980) at 6d0381f19 and 2e2d40cb3, which are after the bump. The test does not assert the facet count on Windows.\n- macOS arm64: NOT measured after the bump. Its pin follows Windows and Linux, because macOS had the same triple before. A macOS run must confirm it.\n\n## Tests\n\n- Quick gate (`cargo xtask gate quick --base origin/main`): `app suites: pocket3d_hsm` ok. This includes `the_linux_x86_64_bore_count_pin_is_exact` and `the_stamp_tells_the_hsm_plan_from_the_rings`.\n- Heavy step (`cargo xtask gate release --only \"app heavy: pocket3d_hsm\"`): ok on Linux x86_64. `the_users_bore_roughs_adaptive_level_by_level_and_verifies_clean` passes. The known-fail #1950 test is skipped by the gate, as before.\n- Light lane (`cargo xtask gate light`): 6 of 6 steps ok.\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-app --test pocket3d_hsm -- -D warnings`: clean.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- The Windows and macOS runs of the updated test. `the_windows_x86_64_bore_count_pin_is_exact` and `the_macos_arm64_bore_count_pin_is_exact` were not compiled on this host.\n- The example catalog suites: no post or toolpath code changed.\n\nChangelog: none\n\nEffort: medium (no escalation)\n\nFixes #3886\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
